**🍵Selection Sort :-**

Selection Sort is a simple, comparison-based sorting algorithm that sorts an array by repeatedly finding the minimum element from the unsorted portion and swapping it with the first element of that unsorted portion.

The algorithm conceptually divides the array into two segments: a sorted subarray built from left to right, and a remaining unsorted subarray

**🍵How It Works**

Find Minimum: Scan the unsorted part of the list to locate the smallest value.

Swap Element: Swap this smallest value with the leftmost element of the unsorted section.

Shift Boundary: Move the imaginary boundary separating the sorted and unsorted sections one step to the right.

Repeat: Continue these steps until the entire array is fully sorted.

**🍵Performance and Complexity**

Time Complexity: O(n^2) for best, average, and worst cases because it uses nested loops to scan the remaining array regardless of its initial order.

Space Complexity: O(1) auxiliary space because it sorts the items directly. It is a strict in-place algorithm.

Stability: Unstable, meaning it can alter the original relative order of duplicate elements during swapping operations.

**🍵Core Advantages and Disadvantages**

Pro: Performs a minimal number of memory writes. It makes a maximum of O(n)swaps, making it highly efficient when write operations are physically costly.

Con: Highly inefficient on large datasets compared to advanced algorithms like Quick Sort or Merge Sort
